1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,447
This tutorial follows 5 percent VAT on a
commercial

2
00:00:03,447 --> 00:00:06,510
tenancy contract, from posting to the VAT
return.

3
00:00:06,510 --> 00:00:10,340
As the Accountant, open Noor Pharmacy's
draft contract for shop

4
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:13,787
BC-G01 at Palm Ridge Business Centre:
150,000 in rent

5
00:00:13,787 --> 00:00:17,233
plus 7,500 of VAT, and a 15,000 security
deposit.

6
00:00:17,233 --> 00:00:20,680
Select Post Contract, check the review,
and post it.

7
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:22,595
Now select View posting journal.

8
00:00:22,595 --> 00:00:26,041
The deposit and the rent are debited to
Rent

9
00:00:26,041 --> 00:00:29,488
Receivable and credited to Security
Deposit and Advance Rent.

10
00:00:29,488 --> 00:00:33,317
The VAT is its own pair: Rent Receivable
is debited

11
00:00:33,317 --> 00:00:37,147
with 7,500, and Output VAT not yet due is
credited.

12
00:00:37,147 --> 00:00:40,594
A tenancy contract is not a tax invoice,
so

13
00:00:40,594 --> 00:00:43,657
posting it does not make the VAT payable.

14
00:00:43,657 --> 00:00:46,721
Back on the contract, open the Cheques
tab.

15
00:00:46,721 --> 00:00:50,168
Each of the four rent cheques of 39,375
carries

16
00:00:50,168 --> 00:00:53,231
1,875 of VAT, and the deposit carries
none.

17
00:00:53,231 --> 00:00:55,529
Each instalment brings its own VAT.

18
00:00:55,529 --> 00:00:58,593
Now open Meridian Logistics' contract,
which has run

19
00:00:58,593 --> 00:01:01,274
for a year, and its VAT section.

20
00:01:01,274 --> 00:01:05,486
There are four tax points: 1 October, 1
January, 1 April

21
00:01:05,486 --> 00:01:09,316
and 1 July, each with its own numbered tax
invoice.

22
00:01:09,316 --> 00:01:12,762
A tax point is the instalment's due date,
or

23
00:01:12,762 --> 00:01:16,209
the day it was paid if that came first.

24
00:01:16,209 --> 00:01:20,039
Open the July journal: it debits Output
VAT not yet

25
00:01:20,039 --> 00:01:23,485
due and credits Output VAT on Sales with
1,500.

26
00:01:23,485 --> 00:01:27,315
Tax points are declared every night, and
on Month-end recognition

27
00:01:27,315 --> 00:01:30,761
you can declare them yourself before
closing a period.

28
00:01:30,761 --> 00:01:34,208
Preview shows what has fallen due: Noor's
first rent

29
00:01:34,208 --> 00:01:37,272
cheque, dated 1 October, with 1,875 of
VAT.

30
00:01:37,272 --> 00:01:39,952
Now open Final Reports, VAT return, and

31
00:01:39,952 --> 00:01:42,250
pick the quarter from July 2026.

32
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:46,080
Box 1b holds the standard-rated rent in
Dubai and its

33
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:49,909
VAT, and box 9 the input VAT on supplier
bills.

34
00:01:49,909 --> 00:01:54,122
The check confirms that the output VAT
ties to the ledger.

35
00:01:54,122 --> 00:01:57,569
Select box 1b to see the documents behind
it.

36
00:01:57,569 --> 00:02:00,249
The quarter ended on 30 September, so

37
00:02:00,249 --> 00:02:02,930
the Company Admin can now file it.

38
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:06,377
The April quarter shows a filed return:
the Company

39
00:02:06,377 --> 00:02:09,440
Admin filed it with the tax authority's
reference,

40
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:12,504
and filing locks VAT dated in that
quarter.
